  "question": {
    "text": "Which one of the following group-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn></math> hydride is the strongest reducing agent ?"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>AsH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BiH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>PH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>SbH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>When we go down the group, the size of the atom increase also the distance between the metal hydrogen bond increases in hydrides. So, the strength of the metal hydrogen bond decreases. A reducing agent is one that supplies a hydrogen atom. Among <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>15</mn><mrow><mi>th</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow></msup></math> group hydrides, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BiH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> is strongest reducing agent.</p>"
  }
